;; Cycles through finalizers (queue 222; doc/python/PYTHON-SURVEY.md, "Design: 222"): Go never frees a cycle that
;; reaches a finalized object from itself, so gc.collect() finds them as CPython's gc_collect_main does, with Go's
;; collector answering what deduce_unreachable's counts answer: which objects are held from outside. The graph's nodes
;; are instances (their dictionary, while not handed out as an object, and __slots__ folded in), lists, tuples and
;; dictionaries; its edges their slots of interface type that hold a node. Every edge between two probed nodes is
;; hidden (the slot set to nil, its target kept by address), each probed node given a probe's Go finalizer, and Go
;; collects with the GIL held: a node whose probe does not run is held from outside, and so is what it reaches.

